menu "User Interface"

config HR_UI_SOURCE_CONTROL
  bool "Front-panel source button and LEDs"
  select GPIO
  default y
  help
    Cycles the active input with the board button (sw0), lights one LED per
    source, and announces the change through the speaker. This is what makes
    the device usable standalone: without it the only way to change source is
    source_manager's automatic health-based fallback, since nothing else calls
    source_manager_select().

    Requires the board to provide the sw0 and led0..led3 aliases, which the
    nRF5340 DK does.

if HR_UI_SOURCE_CONTROL

config HR_UI_AUDIO_CUE
  bool "Announce the active source with a beep pattern"
  default y
  help
    Plays a short pattern identifying the new source: 1 beep for BLE, 2 for
    USB, 3 for AUX, 4 for none. Pitch differs per source as well, so a cue is
    recognisable without counting.

    The cue is written straight to the output backend rather than through
    source_manager, so it is heard whichever input is active. Input frames are
    dropped for its duration so the two are not interleaved.

config HR_UI_DEBOUNCE_MS
  int "Button debounce (ms)"
  default 40
  range 5 250

config HR_UI_STACK_SIZE
  int "UI thread stack size"
  default 2048
  range 1024 8192
  help
    The press is handled on its own thread because announcing the source means
    sleeping for the length of the cue; doing that on the system workqueue
    would stall Bluetooth and USB work items.

config HR_UI_THREAD_PRIORITY
  int "UI thread priority"
  default 7

endif # HR_UI_SOURCE_CONTROL

endmenu
